Build it through @nestjs/testing's module system rather than constructing UsersService by hand -- that way the test exercises the same DI wiring the real app uses, instead of skipping it:

const moduleRef = await Test.createTestingModule({
  providers: [
    UsersService,
    { provide: UsersRepository, useValue: mockRepo },
  ],
})
  .overrideProvider(UsersRepository)
  .useValue(mockRepo)
  .compile();

const service = moduleRef.get(UsersService);

Mock at the UsersRepository boundary (the thing UsersService actually depends on), not by stubbing a private method inside UsersService itself -- that keeps the test coupled to the real dependency contract instead of an implementation detail. Then assert against UsersService's own return values or thrown exceptions using the mockRepo's canned responses.

That's almost always a missing app.close(). An e2e spec that does moduleRef.createNestApplication() and app.init() but never closes the app leaves open handles behind -- DB connections, timers, anything the app opened during bootstrap -- and the test runner can't exit cleanly because those handles are still alive. Fix it by closing the app once the spec file is done:

afterAll(async () => {
  await app.close();
});

Check every e2e spec file that calls createNestApplication() has a matching close in its afterAll, not just the one you're currently debugging -- it's easy for one spec file to have it and a newer one to be missing it.
